  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nIn most cases across the area, not a cloud in the sky. Or at the \nvery least, hardly a cloud in the sky. As broad mid-level ridging is \nbuilding into Central CONUS today, not too much surprise there.\nThis also lends to the ongoing seasonally warm to hot \nconditions. Temperatures early this afternoon are predominantly \nin the low to mid 80s, on their way to mid to upper 80s in most \ncases. A few spots near to south of Interstate 70 may touch the \nlow 90s. This potential too is already evident with sites like \nClinton MO already to 87 degrees. \n\nThe large scale pattern will begin to see significant changes \nthrough the weekend, to include... Hurricane Isaias makes landfall \nlate tonight and gradually drifts northward through the Lower \nMississippi Valley. Hurricane Simon makes landfall along the NW \nMexican Coast late Saturday night. A trough/cutoff low begins to dig \ninto the PNW and down the West Coast, and eventually brings Simon \nremnants into the fold. The general evolution is well corroborated \namong the deterministic synoptic models and their ensembles through \nthe weekend. While Isaias drifts northward post-landfall, the west \ncoast features will progress the pattern such that mid-level ridging \nbuilds into the central CONUs and helps deflect Isaias eastward, \npreventing any sensible effects to the forecast area. With the mid-\nlevel ridging though, well above normal temperatures will continue. \nExpect highs through the weekend similar to today, mid to upper 80s \nand some spots south of Interstate 70 possibly hitting the low 90s. \n\nThe work-week will start off similar to the weekend, if not a couple \nto few degrees warmer in spots as the thermal ridge axis gets pushed \nover the area. With depictions of low 20s deg C, high temperatures a \ncouple to few degrees higher than the weekend are anticipated. This \nmay bring some record highs into consideration, including the KC \nMetro where the existing record Monday is 91 (1899) and the current \nforecast is 90. Moving toward the middle portions of the week, a \nsizable amount of uncertainty remains in the forecast. Namely around \nprecipitation onset and temperatures. This includes high \ntemperatures spreads of 10 deg F or greater both Tuesday and \nWednesday. That said, there appears there may be a gradually growing \nconsensus around a faster and not as deep (latitudinally) western \ntrough. Within the 12z LREF cluster analysis, this is suggested with \n~41% of total members clustering around this prevailing solution and \nan additional ~19% around a similar, albeit slightly slower and \nweaker, solution. Should that prevail, it would tend to push the \nwarm front further north, which in turn would favor warmer \ntemperatures and best precipitation chances further north on \nTuesday. At this time, the NBM and WPC guidance looks to still favor \na cooler and more southerly solution, in part as they would have \nprimarily used the previous couple runs of the LREF. For now, have \nnot deviated from NBM/WPC guidance, with desire to see more run-run \nconfidence and/or continued trend. Regardless,  it will rain within \nthe Tuesday thru Thursday time frame, just a matter of being able to \nnarrow that window some. Of additional note, as the western trough \nkicks out into the Plains, so too will an attendant surface low and \na possible window for strong to severe storms somewhere in the \nregion Wednesday evening / night. This is most evident with the \nstrong wind fields and forcing vs CAPE ... high-shear, low-\nCAPE. This too is reflected in the current SPC Day 5 15 percent \nrisk area.",
